  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 307 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThe trough drifts east through the central Plains Wednesday night \nthrough Thursday night and drags a cool high pressure system out of \nsouthern Canada into the northern Plains and Upper Midwest.  This \npushes the surface front slowly southeast out of Iowa and through \nMissouri and Illinois by 00Z Saturday.  The trough will still have \nresidual tropical moisture from the remnants of hurricane Polo \nwrapped into it, and locally heavy rain is still a possibility. \nHowever, model trends continue to favor northwest Missouri for the \nheaviest rain.  LREF mean precipitable water values drop as the \nfront moves from northwest into central and northeast Missouri \nThursday, and continue to drop slowly through Friday.  The latest \nstorm-total QPF for Wednesday night through Friday night from the \nNBM ranges from about 2.5 inches in northeast Missouri to a little \nover an inch in south central Illinois.  Flash flood guidance is \nlowest where QPF is highest in northeast Missouri, but it's still \n1.75+ inches in 1 hour.  Forecast soundings are still supportive of \nheavy rain with warm cloud depths in excess of 12kft and a deep \nmoist layer aloft.  However there occasionally indications of dry \nair entrainment which would likely eat into rainfall totals, and the \nfront remains progressive through our area which lowers the chance \nfor training.  Bottom line is that occasional heavy rain is possible \ngiven the thermodynamics and remnant tropical moisture, confidence \nthat there will be enough rain to cause flooding is still low at \nthis time.\n\nTemperatures in the rain on Thursday in parts of central and \nnortheast Missouri as well as west central Illinois will drop back \nto near or below normal in the mid to upper 70s.  The remainder of \nthe area will see another warm day in the mid to upper 80s well \nsouth of the front and the more persistent rain and cloud cover. \nCool high pressure builds across the Midwest and Great Lakes Friday \ninto Saturday north of the front.  It also looks like there will be \nlingering clouds and rain, mostly along and south of I-44 in \nMissouri and I-64 in Illinois both Friday and Saturday. Guidance \ntemperatures drop back to near and below normal between the \nlingering precipitation and cooler airmass moving into the region. \nAnother short wave aloft moves across the Upper Midwest and Great \nLakes late in the weekend which brings a reinforcing shot of cool \nair down into the Mississippi Valley late Sunday into Monday.  This \nkeeps temperatures several degrees below normal int the upper 60s to \naround 70 Sunday and Monday.  Could even see temperatures falling \nbelow 50 over a good portion of the forecast area on Monday morning \nas the reinforcing high settles across the Midwest.",
